# Build and call an authenticated MCP server

Start with one tool and a focused test. Then add an ephemeral local issuer,
start a loopback HTTP endpoint, and check discovery, listing, calling, and
refusals. Optional cells introduce the 2.4 request metadata and presentation
APIs.

Run the cells in order in a dedicated Livebook runtime with Elixir 1.18 or
newer. The first cell needs network access to Hex and compiles dependencies;
later cells use an available loopback port and need no external service,
database, Phoenix application, or existing signing key. The package starts
its bounded schema cache and embedded dialects automatically.

The signing key and short-lived tokens stay in this runtime and are not printed.
Run the cleanup cell before rerunning the setup. It stops only the processes
created here and restores the previous static-keystore configuration. Closing
the dedicated runtime also ends these processes.

## Install the released packages

The `~> 2.4` requirement admits 2.4.0 and later compatible 2.x releases. To
repeat this walkthrough against exactly 2.4.0, replace it with `"2.4.0"`.

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:attesto_mcp_server, "~> 2.4"},
    {:bandit, "~> 1.6"},
    {:jason, "~> 1.4"},
    {:jose, "~> 1.11"}
  ],
  verbose: false
)
```

## Start a server and register one tool

A tool receives decoded arguments and an authenticated context. This greeting
tool uses string keys, validates both its input and structured output, and
requires the `mcp:tools:call` scope. `tool_from_context/3` inherits the server's
JSON budget and output settings. In an application, supervise the server under
your application supervisor rather than starting it from a notebook cell.

```elixir
alias AttestoMCP.Server.{API, Content, RequestMeta, Result}
alias AttestoMCP.Server.Test, as: MCPTest

greet = %{
  description: "Return a greeting for the supplied name",
  required_scopes: [AttestoMCP.Scopes.tools_call()],
  input_schema: %{
    "type" => "object",
    "properties" => %{"name" => %{"type" => "string", "minLength" => 1, "maxLength" => 64}},
    "required" => ["name"],
    "additionalProperties" => false
  },
  output_schema: %{
    "type" => "object",
    "properties" => %{"message" => %{"type" => "string"}},
    "required" => ["message"],
    "additionalProperties" => false
  },
  handler: fn %{"name" => name}, context ->
    message = "Hello, #{name}!"

    {:ok,
     Result.tool_from_context(Content.text(message), context,
       structured_content: %{"message" => message}
     )}
  end
}

{:ok, server} = API.start_link()
:ok = API.register_tool(server, "greet", greet)

%{registered_tool: "greet", server_started: Process.alive?(server)}
```

## Test the tool before adding HTTP

These helpers use the real dispatcher, schema checks, definition scopes,
handler, and result validation. Their identity and scopes stand in for an
already authenticated request; they do not verify a token, DPoP proof, mTLS
certificate, or HTTP headers. The HTTP cells below exercise that separate
boundary.

```elixir
test_context = [principal: "usr_notebook", scopes: [AttestoMCP.Scopes.tools_call()]]

%{"result" => %{"structuredContent" => %{"message" => "Hello, reader!"}}} =
  MCPTest.call_tool(server, "greet", %{"name" => "reader"}, test_context)

%{"error" => %{"data" => %{"reason" => "tool_arguments_invalid"}}} =
  MCPTest.call_tool(server, "greet", %{"name" => ""}, test_context)

%{"error" => _denied} =
  MCPTest.call_tool(server, "greet", %{"name" => "reader"}, principal: "usr_notebook")

%{valid_input: :passed, invalid_input: :rejected, missing_definition_scope: :rejected}
```

## Add an ephemeral issuer and HTTP endpoint

This notebook mints a token directly to demonstrate resource-server
verification. `https://auth.example.test` is a synthetic issuer identifier;
there are no OAuth authorization, login, discovery, consent, or token endpoints
at that address. An OAuth-enabled MCP client needs a real authorization server.
Use the Phoenix setup guide when moving to that flow.

The local endpoint uses plain HTTP only on `127.0.0.1`. Production endpoints
need HTTPS and the host's issuer, key, revocation, principal-loading, and sender
constraint policy. The demonstration token is an ordinary bearer token; it does
not demonstrate DPoP or mTLS.

```elixir
{:ok, _bandit_apps} = Application.ensure_all_started(:bandit)
{:ok, _inets_apps} = Application.ensure_all_started(:inets)

restore_keystore =
  case Application.fetch_env(:attesto, Attesto.Keystore.Static) do
    {:ok, previous} -> fn -> Application.put_env(:attesto, Attesto.Keystore.Static, previous) end
    :error -> fn -> Application.delete_env(:attesto, Attesto.Keystore.Static) end
  end

stop_owned = fn pid, stop ->
  if is_pid(pid) and Process.alive?(pid) do
    try do
      stop.(pid)
    catch
      :exit, {:noproc, _call} -> :ok
    end
  end

  :ok
end

cleanup = fn server_pid, bandit_pid ->
  :ok = stop_owned.(bandit_pid, &ThousandIsland.stop/1)
  :ok = stop_owned.(server_pid, &GenServer.stop/1)
  :ok = restore_keystore.()
end

signing_pem = {:ec, "P-256"} |> JOSE.JWK.generate_key() |> JOSE.JWK.to_pem() |> elem(1)
Application.put_env(:attesto, Attesto.Keystore.Static, signing_pem: signing_pem)

%{local_signing_key: :ready, previous_configuration: :saved_for_cleanup}
```

The authentication boundary needs the actual resource URL before Bandit starts.
Reserve a loopback port long enough to read its number, then start the endpoint.
If startup fails, call `cleanup.(server, nil)` before rerunning the setup.

```elixir
{:ok, reservation} =
  :gen_tcp.listen(0, [:binary, active: false, ip: {127, 0, 0, 1}, reuseaddr: true])

{:ok, {{127, 0, 0, 1}, port}} = :inet.sockname(reservation)
:ok = :gen_tcp.close(reservation)

mcp_url = "http://127.0.0.1:#{port}/mcp"

auth_config =
  Attesto.Config.new(
    issuer: "https://auth.example.test",
    audience: mcp_url,
    keystore: Attesto.Keystore.Static,
    principal_kinds: [
      Attesto.PrincipalKind.new("user", "usr_",
        required_claims: [{"client_id", :non_empty_string}]
      )
    ]
  )

mint_token = fn granted_scopes ->
  {:ok, issued} =
    Attesto.Token.mint(
      auth_config,
      %{
        kind: "user",
        sub: "usr_notebook",
        scopes: granted_scopes,
        claims: %{"client_id" => "notebook-client"}
      },
      lifetime: 900
    )

  issued.access_token
end

scopes = [AttestoMCP.Scopes.tools_read(), AttestoMCP.Scopes.tools_call()]
access_token = mint_token.(scopes)
read_only_token = mint_token.([AttestoMCP.Scopes.tools_read()])

{:ok, bandit} =
  Bandit.start_link(
    plug:
      {AttestoMCP.Server.Plug,
       server: server, path: "/mcp", auth: [config: auth_config, resource: mcp_url]},
    scheme: :http,
    ip: {127, 0, 0, 1},
    port: port,
    startup_log: false
  )

%{endpoint: mcp_url, granted_scopes: scopes, token_lifetime_seconds: 900}
```

The temporary socket closes before Bandit binds it. A competing local process
could take that port; if the bind fails, clean up and rerun. If the tokens expire
while reading, copy only `access_token = mint_token.(scopes)` and
`read_only_token = mint_token.([AttestoMCP.Scopes.tools_read()])` into a new cell.
Reevaluate that new cell, then retry the HTTP requests. Do not reevaluate the
whole endpoint cell: it also starts Bandit.

## Discover, list, and call over authenticated HTTP

The modern `2026-07-28` transport is session-free. Each request includes the
protocol version and client capabilities in `_meta`, plus matching protocol,
method, and selected-tool headers. This small JSON request helper is only for
the request/response calls in this notebook; SSE subscriptions and earlier
session-bound revisions need their own transport flow.

```elixir
protocol_version = "2026-07-28"

post_mcp = fn id, method, params, token ->
  meta =
    Map.merge(Map.get(params, "_meta", %{}), %{
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/protocolVersion" => protocol_version,
      "io.modelcontextprotocol/clientCapabilities" => %{}
    })

  body =
    Jason.encode!(%{
      "jsonrpc" => "2.0",
      "id" => id,
      "method" => method,
      "params" => Map.put(params, "_meta", meta)
    })

  headers = [
    {~c"accept", ~c"application/json, text/event-stream"},
    {~c"mcp-protocol-version", String.to_charlist(protocol_version)},
    {~c"mcp-method", String.to_charlist(method)}
  ]

  headers =
    if is_binary(token),
      do: [{~c"authorization", String.to_charlist("Bearer " <> token)} | headers],
      else: headers

  headers =
    if method == "tools/call",
      do: [{~c"mcp-name", String.to_charlist(params["name"])} | headers],
      else: headers

  {:ok, {{_version, status, _reason}, response_headers, response_body}} =
    :httpc.request(
      :post,
      {String.to_charlist(mcp_url), headers, ~c"application/json", body},
      [timeout: 5_000],
      body_format: :binary
    )

  decoded =
    case Jason.decode(response_body) do
      {:ok, value} -> value
      {:error, _} -> nil
    end

  {status, decoded, response_headers}
end

{200, discover, _} = post_mcp.(1, "server/discover", %{}, access_token)
%{"result" => %{"supportedVersions" => supported}} = discover
true = protocol_version in supported

{200, listed, _} = post_mcp.(2, "tools/list", %{}, access_token)
%{"result" => %{"tools" => [%{"name" => "greet"}]}} = listed

arguments = %{"name" => "greet", "arguments" => %{"name" => "reader"}}
{200, called, _} = post_mcp.(3, "tools/call", arguments, access_token)

%{
  "result" => %{
    "resultType" => "complete",
    "structuredContent" => %{"message" => "Hello, reader!"}
  }
} = called

%{discovery: :passed, tool_listing: :passed, tool_call: called["result"]}
```

## Check the authentication and validation refusals

Missing credentials receive HTTP 401 and an OAuth resource-metadata challenge.
A valid token without the tool-call scope receives HTTP 403. Invalid arguments
return a JSON-RPC invalid-params error after authentication; a successful HTTP
exchange alone does not mean the tool succeeded.

```elixir
{401, _missing_token, challenge_headers} = post_mcp.(4, "tools/list", %{}, nil)

true =
  Enum.any?(challenge_headers, fn {name, value} ->
    String.downcase(to_string(name)) == "www-authenticate" and
      String.contains?(to_string(value), "resource_metadata=")
  end)

{403, _missing_scope, _} = post_mcp.(5, "tools/call", arguments, read_only_token)

{200, rejected, _} =
  post_mcp.(6, "tools/call", %{"name" => "greet", "arguments" => %{"name" => ""}}, access_token)

%{"error" => %{"code" => -32602, "data" => %{"reason" => "tool_arguments_invalid"}}} = rejected

%{missing_token: 401, missing_scope: 403, invalid_arguments: -32602}
```

## Optional: read application metadata in 2.4

`context.request_meta` is a bounded snapshot of the current request's untrusted
metadata. `RequestMeta.application/1` excludes protocol-reserved keys. Here it
controls only an explicitly returned diagnostic label; never use metadata to
replace the verified principal, tenant, scopes, issuer, or sender binding.

```elixir
:ok =
  API.register_tool(server, "request_label", %{
    description: "Return an example application metadata label",
    required_scopes: [AttestoMCP.Scopes.tools_call()],
    input_schema: %{"type" => "object", "additionalProperties" => false},
    handler: fn _arguments, context ->
      label = RequestMeta.application(context.request_meta)["com.example/request-label"]

      {:ok,
       Result.tool_from_context(Content.text("Application metadata example"), context,
         structured_content: %{"label" => label}
       )}
    end
  })

%{"result" => %{"structuredContent" => %{"label" => "tutorial"}}} =
  MCPTest.call_tool(
    server,
    "request_label",
    %{},
    test_context ++ [meta: %{"com.example/request-label" => "tutorial"}]
  )

{200, label_response, _} =
  post_mcp.(
    7,
    "tools/call",
    %{
      "name" => "request_label",
      "arguments" => %{},
      "_meta" => %{"com.example/request-label" => "tutorial"}
    },
    access_token
  )

%{"result" => %{"structuredContent" => %{"label" => "tutorial"}}} = label_response

%{metadata_via_test_helper: :passed, metadata_via_http: :passed}
```

## Optional: guidance, presentation, icons, cache hints, and dialect export

These settings are independent of authentication. Guidance and display
callbacks do not grant access to a hidden tool. Cache hints describe client-side
reuse; the server does not cache responses, and protected HTTP stays
`private, no-store`. This example keeps hints private and uses an inline icon,
so no image service is needed.

A second server exercises these startup options without changing the first
server or HTTP endpoint. The `after` block stops it even if an assertion fails.

```elixir
icon =
  "data:image/svg+xml;base64," <>
    Base.encode64(
      ~s|<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="16" height="16"><circle cx="8" cy="8" r="6" fill="blue"/></svg>|
    )

{:ok, feature_server} =
  API.start_link(
    server_name: "notebook-catalog",
    server_icons: [%{src: icon, mime_type: "image/svg+xml", sizes: ["16x16"]}],
    export_schema_dialect: true,
    instructions_provider: fn _context -> {:ok, "Use greet for a short greeting."} end,
    tool_presentation: fn _tool, _context -> {:ok, %{"title" => "Greeting"}} end,
    cache_policy: [methods: %{"server/discover" => [ttl_ms: 1_000, scope: :private]}]
  )

try do
  :ok = API.register_tool(feature_server, "greet", greet)

  %{
    "result" => %{
      "instructions" => "Use greet for a short greeting.",
      "cacheScope" => "private",
      "ttlMs" => 1_000,
      "_meta" => %{
        "io.modelcontextprotocol/serverInfo" => %{
          "name" => "notebook-catalog",
          "icons" => [_icon]
        }
      }
    }
  } = MCPTest.discover(feature_server, test_context)

  %{
    "result" => %{
      "tools" => [
        %{
          "title" => "Greeting",
          "inputSchema" => %{"$schema" => "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema"}
        }
      ]
    }
  } = MCPTest.list_tools(feature_server, test_context)

  %{
    guidance: :passed,
    presentation: :passed,
    icon: :passed,
    private_cache_hint: :passed,
    schema_dialect: :passed
  }
after
  :ok = stop_owned.(feature_server, &GenServer.stop/1)
end
```

The same test-helper family includes resource reads, prompt retrieval,
completion, and catalog pagination. Interactive retries must echo the returned
`requestState` and answer keys, preserving operation parameters and application
metadata. Only tracing, progress, and client display metadata may change. See
the [usage guide](https://hexdocs.pm/attesto_mcp_server/usage.html) for those
contracts and the remaining optional features.

## Clean up and move to an application

This cleanup is safe to run twice. It restores the previous static-keystore
configuration instead of discarding it. Keep this notebook in a dedicated
runtime so other applications do not depend on its temporary key.

```elixir
:ok = cleanup.(server, bandit)
:ok = cleanup.(server, bandit)
false = Process.alive?(server)
false = Process.alive?(bandit)

%{server_stopped: true, http_stopped: true}
```

For a supervised application and real OAuth clients, continue with
[setup](https://hexdocs.pm/attesto_mcp_server/setup.html). A Phoenix host already
using `attesto_phoenix` can reuse its validated authorization configuration with
`mix igniter.install attesto_mcp_server --base-url https://mcp.example.com`.
Review the generated notices, storage migrations, granted scopes, and starter
test. The [usage guide](https://hexdocs.pm/attesto_mcp_server/usage.html) covers
resources, prompts, business policy, DPoP and mTLS integration, sessions,
subscriptions, and deployment limits.
